# Target C-II — convex support-seven H8 cannot have a ground `2+5` sign split

**Date:** 2026-09-19  
**Claim class:** exact metric exclusion inside the degree-pure support-seven endpoint  
**Scope:** convex `H8` branch of `research/target_c_support7_reciprocal_reduction_2026-09-19.md`  
**Depends on:** support-seven reciprocal reduction; reciprocal trace identity; strict triangle inequality; strict convex-quadrilateral crossing inequality; strict negative type of Euclidean distance  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support7_reciprocal_h8_25_metric_exclusion_2026_09_19.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The support-seven reduction leaves 23 convex `H8` sign/order orbits. Exactly two of those orbits have a `2+5` ground force split; equivalently all four labelled sign assignments with ground minority size two belong to those two orbits. Both violate the exact reciprocal signed-length identity.

Therefore every convex `H8` support-seven survivor must have ground split `3+4`, and the convex branch drops from 23 to 21 sign/order orbits.

---

## 1. Metric residuals

Write

\[
d_{ij}=|y_i-y_j|
\]

for the distances among the eight strictly convex reciprocal hull vertices `0,1,...,7`.

We use three families of strict negative linear forms in the distances.

For any three distinct hull vertices, strict triangle inequality gives, for example,

\[
T(a,b,c;bc):=d_{bc}-d_{ab}-d_{ac}<0.
\tag{1.1}
\]

For cyclically ordered `a<b<c<d`, the crossing diagonals `ac,bd` meet in the polygon interior, so

\[
Q_o(a,b,c,d):=d_{ab}+d_{cd}-d_{ac}-d_{bd}<0,
\tag{1.2}
\]

and

\[
Q_x(a,b,c,d):=d_{ad}+d_{bc}-d_{ac}-d_{bd}<0.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Finally, for every nonzero real vector `c=(c_0,...,c_7)` with `sum c_i=0`, strict negative type of the Euclidean metric gives

\[
N(c):=2\sum_{0\le i<j\le7}c_ic_jd_{ij}<0.
\tag{1.4}
\]

The strictness in (1.4) follows from the projection-integral proof already used in the support-five reciprocal exclusion: for a generic one-dimensional projection the projected coordinates are distinct, and the quadratic form is the negative integral of a nonzero squared partial-sum function.

Distances across missing reciprocal edges are allowed in these auxiliary residuals. They carry no reciprocal stress, but their coefficients cancel in the identities below.

---

## 2. First `2+5` orbit

Take the labelled missing 7-cycle

\[
L_A=\{12,17,23,34,45,56,67\}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

One representative positive reciprocal-edge set is

\[
\begin{aligned}
P_A=\{&01,02,04,06,07,13,15,24,26,35,37,46,57\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.2}
\]

The ground sign word on `1,...,7` is `++-+-++`, so its minority size is two. Put

\[
L_A^*=\sum_{e\in P_A}d_e-
\sum_{e\in E(K_8\setminus L_A)\setminus P_A}d_e.
\tag{2.3}
\]

The reciprocal trace identity requires `L_A^*=0`.

The companion certificate verifies the following exact coefficient identity over all 28 pair distances:

\[
-L_A^*=\sum_{r=1}^{19}\alpha_r R_r,
\qquad \alpha_r>0,
\tag{2.4}
\]

where the 19 residuals are three strict triangle residuals, nine strict convex-quadrilateral residuals, and seven strict negative-type residuals. The positive coefficients are in `{1/16,1/8,1/4,1/2}` and are stored explicitly in the verifier using exact rational arithmetic.

Every `R_r<0`, hence

\[
-L_A^*<0,
\qquad L_A^*>0,
\tag{2.5}
\]

contradicting the trace identity.

Global sign reversal sends `L_A^*` to `-L_A^*`, so it is excluded as well.

---

## 3. Second `2+5` orbit

Take

\[
L_B=\{14,15,25,26,36,37,47\}.
\tag{3.1}
\]

A representative positive edge set is

\[
\begin{aligned}
P_B=\{&01,03,04,05,07,12,16,23,27,34,45,56,67\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.2}
\]

Its ground sign word is `+-+++-+`, again with minority size two. Define `L_B^*` by the same positive-minus-negative signed length sum.

Here the exact contradiction is short. The verifier checks

\[
\boxed{
-L_B^*=
T(0,2,5;25)
+Q_x(0,1,4,5)
+Q_o(0,2,4,7)
+Q_x(2,3,6,7)
+\frac12N(1,-1,1,-1,1,-1,1,-1).
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

Every term on the right is strictly negative. Thus `L_B^*>0`, again contradicting exact reciprocal trace cancellation. The globally complemented sign assignment is excluded simultaneously.

---

## 4. Consequence

The support-seven `H8` census had exactly four labelled assignments with ground minority size two. They form precisely the two reflection/global-sign orbits above. Both are impossible. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
H8\Longrightarrow\text{ground force split }3+4.
}
\tag{4.1}
\]

and the convex branch now contains

\[
\boxed{21}
\tag{4.2}
\]

unexcluded sign/order orbits rather than 23.

This is not a support-seven exclusion. The remaining 21 convex orbits still have to be tested against ground force closure, the trace/tensor identities, and the full pentagon angle equations. The nonconvex `H6/H7` branches also remain.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** exact strict-metric certificates exclude both convex support-seven `H8` orbits with ground `2+5`; every surviving convex `H8` candidate has ground `3+4`; 21 convex sign/order orbits remain. C-II remains open.
